Dear Editors,

Over the past months I have developed a desktop application for 3D
printing: Solidon3D. It closes the gap between "model downloaded" and
"part fits" =E2=80=94 without having to learn a CAD program first.

Four routes lead to a finished part:

* Adapt =E2=80=94 import someone else's model, repair defects automatically,
  place a hole, widen a cutout
* Construct =E2=80=94 rebuild from named dimensions and tested building block=
s:
  nut traps, heat-set inserts, threads, hinges, cable grommets
* Generate =E2=80=94 create a model from text or an image and run it through =
the
  repair chain
* Sculpt =E2=80=94 model organic shapes freely

Nothing about this is final: every step stays in the history as an
operation and can be changed at any time. Change one dimension and the
model recomputes =E2=80=94 even twenty steps later. On top of that: layer
analysis with overhangs, islands and support requirements, tolerances
taken from material profiles, and export to STL, 3MF and STEP.

The AI sits on top and is optional: you can describe what you want in the
chat instead of clicking through menus. On request it runs entirely
locally on your own graphics card =E2=80=94 that needs additional software, b=
ut no
data goes to the cloud. Without AI, without an account and without an
internet connection, everything else remains fully usable. Geometry is
always computed by code, never by the language model.

You can take a look here: https://solidon3d.de/en/

The current release is a complete demo =E2=80=94 free, no account, usable unt=
il
the end of October. Windows 10/11, around 173 MB; the macOS and Linux package=
s are following.

